<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of Aphaia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of Aphaia is a Doric temple on the island of Aegina, dedicated to a local goddess and built around 500 BCE. Its pediment sculptures, showing scenes of the Trojan wars, mark the transition from Archaic to Classical Greek sculpture. They were removed in 1811, bought by Crown Prince Ludwig of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and are now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glyptothek">Glyptothek</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-aphaia.jpg" alt="Temple of Aphaia" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paweł 'pbm' Szubert · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aegina_-_Temple_of_Aphaia_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The temple stands on a peak of about 160 m on the eastern side of Aegina, some 13 km by road east of the main port. Aphaia was worshipped only at this sanctuary, and the building was long known as the Temple of Jupiter Panhellenius. Large numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> figurines, many of them female, suggest an open-air sanctuary to a fertility and farming deity, perhaps in continuous use from the 14th century BCE, which some take as a sign of a Minoan connection.</p><p>An altar of about 700 BCE marks the first known building phase, and an inscription of the mid-7th century BCE records a house built for Aphaia by a priest. A first stone temple, dated before 570 BCE, had a cella with two levels of columns and a back room, or adyton. It burned, with much of the sanctuary, around 510 BCE, and its remains were packed into the fill of a new terrace, where they kept traces of paint.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The new temple is a Doric building with a colonnade of six by twelve columns on a platform of 15.5 by 30.5 m, and all but three of its outer columns are monolithic. Inside, two rows of five columns carried a second level reaching the roof. Sphinxes stood at the corners of the roof, whose tiles and antefixes were of marble. Proposed dates run from 510 to 470 BCE.</p><p>Each pediment had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> at its centre among combatants, fallen warriors and arms, celebrating the heroes of Aegina. The western pediment, in Archaic style, showed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> told by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, with Ajax, son of Telamon, prominent. The eastern, thought to be later and closer to Classical work, showed the earlier war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> against King Laomedon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, with Telamon at his side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Pausanias mentioned the site briefly in the 2nd century CE, and the Antiquities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> made the temple known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> in 1797. In 1811 the English architect Charles Robert Cockerell and Baron Otto Magnus von Stackelberg took the fallen pediment figures. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> the Danish sculptor Bertel Thorvaldsen restored them, and they shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoclassicism">Neoclassicism</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-von-klenze">Leo von Klenze</a>. The German School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> excavated the site in the 20th century, first under Adolf Furtwängler, and a campaign under Dieter Ohly from 1966 found substantial remains of the older temple in 1969.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Temple of Aphaia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its merchants traded at Naucratis in Egypt, and its oligarchic elite built the temple of <b>Aphaia</b>, whose pediment sculptures are now in Munich.</blockquote><a href="/state/aegina">Aegina</a></li></ul></section>
